			<outline text="readme.md">
				<outline text="# BonBon">
					<outline text="&lt;i&gt;Writing is so important it deserves its own web.&lt;/i&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What is BonBon?">
					<outline text="BonBon is the first browser for the Markdown web.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It's an app that runs on MacOs and Linux. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Who">
					<outline text="I am Dave Winer, the developer of BonBon, with the help of Claude Code.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="This doc, and all other docs on this site were written by humans, unless otherwise stated.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### How to">
					<outline text="To get started, get the app from the [Download page](download/). ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Docs">
					<outline text="Docs are in the [Docs folder](docs). ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Markdown hosting">
					<outline text="GitHub is probably the best place to get free Markdown hosting. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="What we really need are a few simple desktop apps that publish Markdown files to publicly-accessible internet servers. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Open source" isComment="true">
					<outline text="BonBon will be released in open source as soon as things settle down.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Questions, comments">
					<outline text="We're using the [issues section](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/issues) of the GitHub repository for support. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Where we're going">
					<outline text="We're going to keep developing BonBon, and will work with developers who want to produce products that interop.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="download:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Download BonBon">
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Macintosh download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/mac.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It contains one file, BonBon.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="When you run the app, the Mac OS will require that you approve the download before you can run it.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Note: The Mac build runs only on Apple silicon.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Linux download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/linux.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It contains one folder, BonBon, which contains the program plus the files it needs beside it.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Launch it as you would any other desktop app.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Note: The Linux build runs only on Intel-type chips.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Windows download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/windows.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It contains one folder, BonBon, which contains the program plus the files it needs beside it.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Open the folder and double-click on BonBon.exe. There's no installer. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The first time, Windows shows a blue box, &quot;Windows protected your PC&quot;. Click More info, then Run anyway.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It's for 64-bit Windows 10 or 11.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It has not been tested, if you try it, [let us know](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/issues/21) how it works by posting a comment in Issues. " isComment="true">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Raspberry download">
					<outline text="Download and expand [this zip file](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/releases/latest/download/raspberry.zip).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Run the program named BonBon in the folder.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It needs a 64-bit Raspberry Pi system. A 32-bit one can't run it. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It has not been tested, if you try it, [let us know](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/issues/21) how it works by posting a comment in Issues. " isComment="true">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Docs">
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Background">
					<outline text="Claude and I started writing docs and didn't like anything we produced, and it was very slow going, but I decided to write the docs myself because I had delegated it to Claude for an earlier project and found the docs were so verbose and technical that I felt they were ineffective.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="So we tried a different approach with BonBon. We assumed that users had access to an AI app like Claude or ChatGPT. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="I asked Claude to write a handoff document for another instance of itself re how to help a new user find the features in BonBon that they're looking for.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The doc Claude wrote is so good that humans probably should at least skim it after trying BonBon for a bit on their own. It's really well written imho, and concise, even though when I gave it its instructions I didn't require it to be. This is how Claude talks to one of its own.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Caveat: If we add more files to this folder, unless otherwise stated, will be written by machine. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Basic AI skill doc">
					<outline text="This is the [handoff doc](bonbonSkillFromClaude.md) that I asked  Claude to write it for other AI apps, but it also will be useful to humans ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Hello world">
					<outline text="How to [create](helloworld.md) a Markdown page and view it in BonBon.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:helloworld.md">
				<outline text="# Hello world">
					<outline text="A few short steps to create a Markdown file for viewing in BonBon.">
						<outline text="1. Create a new Gist on GitHub. Name it hello.md. It must end in .md in order for this to work.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="2. Enter some text. Use Markdown code if you like. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="3. In the bottom right [corner](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/gistEditorScreenshot.png) of the window, choose Create public gist. Click the button. A new page displays showing a rendering of your markdown, still within GitHub. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="4. In the upper right corner of the window, [click Raw](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/pointingAtRaw.png). That takes us to the plain Markdown source code at a long URL, visible in the browser address bar. Copy the [URL](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/scripting/1ae7ab5a8da6accbc4202274cc4301ae/raw/18d88689d1cb43e886f5f24a2db3016aacdf997b/hello.md) to the clipboard.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="5. Bring BonBon to the front, paste the URL into its address bar and press return. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="6. If all went well you'll see something [like this](https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/11/16/victory.png). ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="### Notes">
						<outline text="You can put the Markdown file anywhere as long as you have a public web address for it. From there you can build structures of Markdown files, to form a web site. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="You may want to edit the URL that GitHub gives you, in step 4, when you click on the Raw link, so that it continues to reflect any edits you make to the document. To do so, remove the second long string of letters and numbers from the raw URL. It always gives the current version. Applying this method to the URL returned in step 4, it would become [this](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/scripting/1ae7ab5a8da6accbc4202274cc4301ae/raw/hello.md) shorter url. After an edit, the shorter URL can take a few minutes to show the change.">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:charter.md">
				<outline text="# Charter">
					<outline text="When we began work on mdBrowser, which eventually became BonBon, I had a conversation with Claude over a few days to establish a charter for the project because while it is a web browser, it's quite different from the browsers that grew out of the invention of the web by Tim Berners-Lee. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="A few big differences: We display Markdown files not HTML files. There is no place for styling in the Markdown files we show users, but the users can apply style sheets, and we supply five example style sheets with the product. Same with JavaScript, the content has no ability to run script code on the client, but we will add features that let the reader add functionality to the browser via programming. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="As we went along, Claude added to the document. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="So while this was a collaborative effort, the remaining text on this page was written by Claude. One of our principles is that text you read here is human-written unless otherwise stated. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Dave Winer, November 2026">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What it is">
					<outline text="A browser for a web made of Markdown files. It is a pure reading environment: the writer's words, with nothing between them and the reader.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What a page is">
					<outline text="A page is a Markdown file, fetched by URL.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Any HTML in the file is stripped before rendering.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="No CSS. A page can't style itself. The browser decides how it looks.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="No JavaScript. A page is just a document to be read, not an app.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Both HTTP and HTTPS are supported">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### User in control">
					<outline text="The content has no power over CSS and JavaScript, but the user has full access to change the appearance, and even automate and add new features.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### How a page is identified">
					<outline text="The browser sends Accept: text/markdown, text/x-opml with every request for a page, so a server can return Markdown to this browser and HTML to others from the same URL.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="If the server says the content type is text/markdown, it's a page.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="If the server says text/plain or a generic binary type, and the path ends in .md or .markdown, it's a page.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="If the server returns HTML or XML, the browser shows the source, as text. A link to an HTML page, clicked in a page, opens in the reader's historic browser.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="If the server returns a picture, the browser shows the picture.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Anything else is a file, offered for download.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="If the page can't be had, a dialog says why, in the server's words when it gave any.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Images">
					<outline text="Images are allowed and are included in the page.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Formats: PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP. No SVG.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The format is determined by the image file's actual content, not its extension.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### The shell">
					<outline text="Markdown inside the shell, around the edges there is an address bar, history and bookmarks menus, and icons in the browser chrome. Icons come from Font Awesome.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Tabs. Each tab holds one page. Clicking a link opens it in the same tab; Cmd-click opens it in a new tab.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Front matter">
					<outline text="A page can begin with a block of YAML between two lines of three dashes. It holds facts about the page: title, author, pubDate, description, and whatever else the writer wants there.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It isn't part of the text. It comes off before the Markdown is rendered.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="In the text, {{page.title}} is replaced with the title from the front matter, and the same for any other value.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Stylesheets">
					<outline text="The reader chooses how pages look, from the stylesheets that come with the browser.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The reader can add stylesheets of their own.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Table of contents">
					<outline text="A site can have a table of contents, an OPML outline at /.well-known/toc.opml.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="When a site has one, the browser shows arrows on each page that go to the page before it and the page after it.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What a site says about itself">
					<outline text="A site can have a file of settings at /.well-known/bonbon.json.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="One of them says where the browser can listen for changes. When a page changes on the server, it's updated in the window.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Replaceable parts">
					<outline text="The Markdown interpreter">
						<outline text="* The shell talks to it through a small defined interface, so a different one can be dropped in.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* We use standard open source packages for converting Markdown to HTML, and there will be an API that allows other software to handle the conversion.">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="Our runtime engine">
						<outline text="* We build on Electron with a pinned Chromium, used only for display.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* We don't update it on Google's schedule, and it can be swapped out later.">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Source of truth">
					<outline text="The source of truth is the web. When a question comes up about how the browser should behave, the answer is what the web does.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="docs:webhistory.md">
				<outline text="# BonBon: A Historical Perspective">
					<outline text="Last week I had an interesting conversation with ChatGPT about where BonBon fits into the history of the web. I was surprised how quickly it understood the full picture. It started filling in the blanks, so I asked it to summarize the story for others to read. That's what this document contains. DW">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **BonBon goes back to the beginning of the Web.** In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee (TBL) proposed a simple, open system of linked documents. Anyone could publish, anyone could link, and anyone could invent new uses for it. ">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **The original Web was remarkably open-ended.** There were few distinctions between kinds of documents. If you could imagine something, you could probably build it. The Web was a common environment for experimentation, not a collection of applications with predefined capabilities.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **HTML was one of the original design choices.** There's nothing inherently wrong with HTML. It was a practical solution to the problem of representing linked documents. But it was a choice, not an inevitable feature of the Web.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **Over 30 years, enormous complexity accumulated around HTML.** CSS, JavaScript, frameworks, proprietary platforms, and commercial interests transformed the Web. What began as a simple publishing environment became a collection of increasingly specialized systems.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **The Web became fragmented into different kinds of content.** A tweet, a blog post, a social media comment, and a novel are treated as fundamentally different things, often with different editors, rules, limitations, and distribution systems. These distinctions are largely imposed by applications and platforms, not by the underlying architecture of the Web.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **BonBon revisits the original choice of document format.** It asks a counterfactual question: What if TBL had chosen Markdown instead of HTML? Markdown didn't exist in 1989, but today it offers a way to explore that alternative history.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **In BonBon, every document has the same capabilities.** A one-sentence message and a thousand-page novel are both Markdown documents. Neither is a lesser form of the other. A short message can become a long essay; an essay can become a book. The format doesn't dictate what the author is allowed to create.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **BonBon redistributes responsibility between authors and readers.** Authors control the content and have access to the full expressive range of Markdown. Readers and reading software control presentation. The document doesn't have to prescribe its appearance.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **BonBon preserves the fundamental architecture of the Web.** URLs, HTTP, hyperlinks, independent publishing, and interoperability remain. BonBon doesn't require inventing a replacement for the Internet or abandoning the existing Web.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **BonBon represents a new branch, not a new version.** Web 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 suggest successive generations. BonBon is different. It branches from the original ideas behind the Web, following a path that was never taken, while remaining connected to existing infrastructure.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="- **The goal isn't to replace HTML. It's to recover something the Web lost.** The original Web gave individuals enormous freedom to create, publish, link, and experiment without permission. BonBon attempts to restore that freedom by making the basic building block of the Web simple and universal again.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="**The central idea:** BonBon doesn't try to fix 30 years of accumulated Web complexity. It returns to the original architecture, makes a different choice about document representation, and explores what becomes possible as a result.">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="code:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Source code release">
					<outline text="First release contains the code for the Electron apps on Nov 20, 2026. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="To build and run either a Mac or Linux app, this is what you do.">
						<outline text="* Requires a Node install, version 18.20.8 is what we build it with.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Download the code folder, open it in a terminal window.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* npm install">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* npm start">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="If you have questions or comments, add an [Issues](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/issues) post. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="demos:handlers:" isComment="true">
				<outline text="txt">
					<outline text="worknotes.md">
						<outline text="11/19/26; 5:24:42 PM by DW">
							<outline text="plain text files shown as paragraphs in your stylesheet, where today they’re a monospaced listing.">
							</outline>
							<outline text="code written by claude code, deployed locally by dave.">
							</outline>
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="handler.js">
						<outline text="function handler (options, callback) {">
							<outline text="var htmltext = &quot;&quot;;">
							</outline>
							<outline text="function encodeHtml (theText) {">
								<outline text="return (theText.replace (/&amp;/g, &quot;&amp;amp;&quot;).replace (/&lt;/g, &quot;&amp;lt;&quot;).replace (/&gt;/g, &quot;&amp;gt;&quot;));">
								</outline>
								<outline text="}">
								</outline>
							</outline>
							<outline text="options.pagetext.split (/\n\s*\n/).forEach (function (theParagraph) {">
								<outline text="if (theParagraph.trim ().length &gt; 0) {">
									<outline text="htmltext += &quot;&lt;p&gt;&quot; + encodeHtml (theParagraph.trim ()) + &quot;&lt;/p&gt;\n&quot;;">
									</outline>
									<outline text="}">
									</outline>
								</outline>
								<outline text="});">
								</outline>
							</outline>
							<outline text="callback (undefined, {htmltext});">
							</outline>
							<outline text="}">
							</outline>
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="demos:bloginmarkdown:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Scripting News as a Markdown site">
					<outline text="We started with the [OPML archive](https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/tree/master/blog/opml) in the Scripting-News GitHub repo, and then keep it current with the RSS 2.0 feed. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We also use [front matter](https://www.markdowntools.com/blog/markdown-frontmatter-metadata-complete-guide), and are compatible with the major static site generators, and macros. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="You can view Dave's blog viewed in BonBon [here](https://demo.bonbonweb.org/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdave.writersweb.org%2Fscripting%2Findex.md).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Click [here](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/raw/main/demos/bloginmarkdown/scripting.zip) to download the archive. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="demos:brentbloginmarkdown:readme.md">
				<outline text="# Brent's blog as a Markdown site">
					<outline text="[Brent Simmons](https://inessential.com/what-i-do.html)' long-running blog is [inessential.com](https://inessential.com/). The Markdown site we built from his source has all the same content, but adds special features for blogs you read in the BonBon browser. Of course it can be viewed in any Markdown-aware browser. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We started with the Markdown [archive](https://github.com/brentsimmons/inessential.com) Brent maintains on GitHub, and from that ran the same Node app Claude wrote for Scripting News. We are following his RSS 2.0 feed for new posts and updates. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="You can view Brent's blog viewed in BonBon [here](https://demo.bonbonweb.org/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbrent.writersweb.org%2F).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Click [here](https://github.com/scripting/bonBon/raw/main/demos/brentbloginmarkdown/inessential.zip) to download the archive. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</outline>
		<outline text="build script">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildBonBonRepo ()">
			</outline>
		</outline>
		</body>
	</opml>
